## ISSUE 9 — RECEIVING SAME BATCH AT DIFFERENT COST MAY OVERWRITE HISTORICAL COST

### Investigate

Inspect:

`server/services/purchaseService.ts`

Investigate batch creation and merging logic.

Potential issue:

If a purchase receipt matches an existing batch by medicine, batch number, and expiry date, the existing batch quantity may be increased while the purchase cost is overwritten with the latest purchase cost.

This can incorrectly assign the latest acquisition cost to all remaining stock in the batch.

### Example

First receipt:

- 100 units
- Cost: ৳2.00 per unit

Second receipt:

- 100 units
- Cost: ৳2.50 per unit

If both receipts merge into one batch and cost becomes ৳2.50, the system may incorrectly value all 200 units at ৳2.50.

### Required fix

First inspect the existing costing model.

Do not make an unnecessary architecture change if historical cost snapshots and stock layers already correctly preserve actual costs.

If the defect is confirmed, choose a compatible approach.

Preferred options:

### Option A — Separate stock cost layers

Maintain separate receipt cost layers, even when physical batch number and expiry are identical.

Each layer must preserve:

- Receipt reference
- Quantity received
- Remaining quantity
- Unit acquisition cost
- Purchase reference
- Batch number
- Expiry date

### Option B — Weighted-average costing

If the existing system intentionally merges batches, calculate weighted-average cost correctly.

Formula:

\[
\text{Weighted Average Cost} =
\frac{
(Q_1 \times C_1) + (Q_2 \times C_2)
}{
Q_1 + Q_2
}
\]

Use Decimal arithmetic.

### Mandatory

- Never simply overwrite historical batch cost with the latest receipt cost if existing stock has a different acquisition cost.
- Preserve historical sale COGS snapshots.
- Preserve purchase records.
- Preserve batch traceability.
- Avoid migrations that destroy historical costing data.

### Tests required

- Same batch, same cost.
- Same batch, different cost.
- Different expiry.
- Partial stock remaining.
- Sale from merged stock.
- COGS remains correct.
- Stock valuation remains correct.
- Purchase history remains correct.

