# 6. NUMERIC PRECISION POLICY — PRESERVE

Do not weaken or replace the current Decimal-based numerical policy.

The authoritative policy should be:

## Money

- BDT input precision: maximum 2 decimal places.
- Reject excessive input precision.
- Do not silently round user input.
- Explicitly define rounding for derived calculations.

## Quantity

- Maximum 6 decimal places.
- Maximum 18 integer digits.
- No silent rounding.
- Reject overflow.

## Unit conversion

- Decimal arithmetic.
- Maximum 6 decimal places for direct conversion ratios.
- Snapshot conversion values on transactions.
- Historical transactions must not change when medicine conversion configuration changes.

## Cost

- Decimal arithmetic.
- Preserve historical cost snapshots.
- Do not use binary floating point for authoritative financial values.

## Database

Investigate existing SQLite `REAL` conversions and casts.

Do not blindly remove every cast.

Determine whether the cast affects:

- Stock
- Cost
- Financial balances
- Reporting
- Sorting
- Filtering

Correct only the places where binary floating-point conversion creates authoritative calculation or comparison risks.

Preserve backward compatibility and existing data.

